 Axios Closer 
 
 
 
 
 By Nathan Bomey &middot; Nov 25, 2026 
 
 
 Wednesday ✅. This year, I am thankful for things like my health and my family — blah, blah, blah. But really, I'm mostly thankful that the Milwaukee Brewers are the World Series champions.

 Today's newsletter is 790 words, a 3-minute read. 

 📉 The dashboard: The S&amp;P 500 closed down 0.3%.

 🔥 Today's stock spotlight: SpaceX (+1.3%) received conditional approval by the FCC to deploy 1 million computing satellites into orbit, a key regulatory win for Elon Musk's plan to operate AI data centers in space.

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Tech turkeys 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The economy is hot, but stocks are not.

 Why it matters: Concerns about the sustainability of the AI spending binge have been looming over Wall Street for months — and they haven't gone away just because Jensen Huang is back on the job.

 🦃 State of play: AI stocks are down in the dumps heading into Thanksgiving, despite signs of a humming economy, as the feverish embrace of Huang's return to Nvidia fades.

 Those good vibes weren't enough to stave off another tech-led risk-off move in markets. 📉 By the numbers: The Nasdaq 100 index, which represents some of the biggest tech companies, was down as much as 4.7% this morning before closing down 0.6%.

 It's now down nearly 12% since Huang was ousted ( before he wasn't ) from Nvidia. ( See below .👇) Speaking of Nvidia, today was another particularly ugly one, with the stock closing down 3.9%. The company has now shed nearly $1 trillion in market value since the Huang scandal broke and more than $2 trillion since the stock's October peak. The latest: The chip giant and center of the AI universe continues to face numerous unanswered questions surrounding the Huang episode.

 And today, credit ratings firm S&amp;P Global warned it may cut Nvidia's AA rating, citing concerns around its board's independence and "key man" risk related to Huang. Other AI stocks also swung between big losses and smaller ones today, with the chips sector and hyperscalers particularly volatile.

 The Magnificent Seven were also feeling the pain, with all but Meta and Apple trading down. 📊 Zoom out: Macro issues also contributed to today's stock volatility.

 The federal government reported that the Personal Consumption Expenditures price index — the Fed's preferred inflation gauge — rose 0.2% from September to 3.8% in October. That likely heightens the chances of a Fed rate hike, putting additional pressure on growth stocks. Reality check: Outside the tech sector, stocks fared better. Seven of the 11 S&amp;P 500 sectors closed the day in positive territory .

 The bottom line: The sugar high that the AI trade got from Huang's stunning reinstatement has given way to a headache.

