 2. 🎁 Black Friday shows signs of life 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Maybe Black Friday isn't dead after all.

 State of play: The annual post-Thanksgiving shopping extravaganza has declined in significance in recent years as deals have stretched earlier and earlier in the calendar.

 But this year, 87% of consumers expect to shop from Black Friday through Cyber Monday, "marking the highest levels since 2016," according to Deloitte's survey of 1,200 consumers. One reason: Budget-conscious shoppers are holding out for better deals . By the numbers: Black Friday slots into an overall holiday shopping season that's expected to top $1 trillion for the first time, according to the National Retail Federation and Bain &amp; Co .

 Online, consumers spent $82.5 billion in the first 22 days of November, up 9.1% from a year earlier, according to Adobe. 💭 Nathan's thought bubble: I have fond memories of covering the golden days of Black Friday shopping, and I wonder if the nostalgia factor is giving the day a boost again.

 
 
